{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.tool_agent` says TBV owns four things for its ask builder:\n- the model conversation,\n- the supported backend integrations,\n- tool dispatch,\n- structured submission.\n\nThe detailed behaviours sit in refining requirements: `isolation`, `provider_failure`, `submission.*` and `fixed_tools`. Those are assessed under their own requirements and are not reassessed here.\n\n**Conversation.**\n- `supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport` runs a real HTTP exchange over several turns. It asserts that the first request of each call has no `function_call_output`. It asserts that the second request carries the tool output (`a source snapshot`) and the \"Finish by calling submit_result\" instruction.\n- `supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat` makes the same assertions for turn 2 over `/api/chat`. It checks that the tool message holds the delivered result.\n- `behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer` asserts the system message, the user message order and the typed result.\n\n**Supported backends (`AgentConfig` has Bedrock, OpenAI and Ollama).**\n- OpenAI and Ollama are tested end to end against local TCP servers. The tests assert the endpoint path, the model, the auth header (Ollama), the options (`max_output_tokens`, `num_predict`, `stream:false`), the tool list and the submit schema.\n- Bedrock has no end-to-end conversation test. Its integration pieces are tested directly:\n  - `bedrock_native_option_types_are_checked_and_only_max_tokens_is_defaulted`\n  - `cache_checkpoints_are_requested_for_caching_models`\n  - `typed_client_calls_preserve_order_and_native_history`\n- A Bedrock end-to-end test would need the AWS service. The project does not stand one up, and `RigProvider::connect` builds a real AWS SDK client. I treat that as the network-service exemption, and the Bedrock-specific logic is covered at the helper level.\n\n**Tool dispatch.**\n- `application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation` mixes graph tools and an application tool in one turn. It asserts every tool is advertised, the results are delivered per call, and the paging and edge outputs are correct.\n- `application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call` checks that per-call tool state is fresh and that tools are advertised.\n- `source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads` dispatches `search_text` and `read_source` and checks the dependencies recorded.\n\n**Structured submission.**\n- The OpenAI and Ollama tests assert the `submit_result` schema (`required: [\"result\"]`, boolean type) and that a submitted `true` becomes the answer.\n- The behavioural adequacy test checks the typed structured result and its schema.\n\n**Isolation of concurrent conversations.**\n- `concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads` uses a barrier so two conversations must run concurrently. It asserts that each gets its own answer, call counts and dependency set.\n- `agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces` asserts the same for per-execution histories and dependency traces.\n\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behaviour of the umbrella obligation that these tests would accept. Each test carries a `{test}` reference to the subject. No acknowledgments apply, and none are needed.",
  "references": "152e27f9c9e7bc9c3e830b7fbfb7088f5da2327804c01b46add84e64cd3e0136",
